    "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/Seattle-University/Associate-Director--Employer-and-Experiential-Partnerships_JR100384": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Employer and Experiential Partnerships",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary The Associate Director provides leadership for the development, management, and stewardship of employer and industry partnerships that expand access to internships, employment, experiential learning, mentorship, recruiting programs, and other career-connected opportunities for Seattle University students and recent alumni. Reporting to the Executive Director of Career Engagement, the Associate Director translates the university’s institutional employer engagement strategy into coordinated outreach, partnership development, recruiting activity, and measurable student opportunities. The position manages the full lifecycle of employer engagement from prospect identification and relationship cultivation through activation, stewardship, assessment, and renewal. The Associate Director works closely with Career Engagement colleagues, the Albers Career Center, faculty, experiential learning partners, Alumni Engagement, Advancement, Student Employment, and other campus stakeholders to support an integrated university-wide career ecosystem. The successful candidate will demonstrate a commitment to Seattle University’s Jesuit Catholic mission, professional formation, social justice, equity, inclusion, cultural humility, and student success. Position Description: The Associate Director provides leadership for the development, management, and stewardship of employer and industry partnerships that expand access to internships, employment, experiential learning, mentorship, recruiting programs, and other career-connected opportunities for Seattle University students and recent alumni. Reporting to the Executive Director of Career Engagement, the Associate Director translates the university’s institutional employer engagement strategy into coordinated outreach, partnership development, recruiting activity, and measurable student opportunities. The position manages the full lifecycle of employer engagement from prospect identification and relationship cultivation through activation, stewardship, assessment, and renewal. The Associate Director works closely with Career Engagement colleagues, the Albers Career Center, faculty, experiential learning partners, Alumni Engagement, Advancement, Student Employment, and other campus stakeholders to support an integrated university-wide career ecosystem. The successful candidate will demonstrate a commitment to Seattle University’s Jesuit Catholic mission, professional formation, social justice, equity, inclusion, cultural humility, and student success. Essential Functions Employer and Industry Partnership Development - 35% Develop and maintain a diverse portfolio of employer, industry, government, nonprofit, and community partnerships aligned with student interests, academic programs, workforce trends, and institutional priorities. Identify and cultivate new employers that can provide internships, entry-level employment, project-based learning, mentorship, job-shadowing, career treks, classroom engagement, and other experiential opportunities. Build employer pipelines across industries and occupational areas, with particular attention to sectors with strong regional demand and relevance to Seattle University students. Meet with employers to understand workforce needs and develop customized engagement strategies that connect organizations with Seattle University students, faculty, academic programs, and university initiatives. Advise employers on recruiting practices, internship program development, employer branding, campus visibility, and inclusive approaches to attracting and retaining early-career talent. Proactively cultivate organizations committed to creating equitable access to employment and experiential learning for first-generation students, students of color, students with disabilities, international students, veterans, and other historically underrepresented populations. Maintain awareness of labor-market conditions, emerging industries, workforce trends, recruiting practices, and changing employer expectations. Represent Seattle University and Career Engagement at employer meetings, professional associations, chambers of commerce, workforce-development conferences, and community events. Employer Stewardship and Partnership Activation - 20% Manage the full employer relationship lifecycle, including cultivation, onboarding, activation, stewardship, assessment, renewal, and expansion. Develop engagement plans for priority employers and ensure consistent communication and follow-through across the university. Translate employer interest into specific opportunities such as internships, jobs, mentoring relationships, sponsored projects, career events, classroom presentations, site visits, and experiential learning partnerships. Coordinate with academic colleges and campus partners to connect employer opportunities with appropriate students, programs, faculty, and student organizations. Support college-based career and professional formation leaders by providing access to employer relationships, labor-market insights, and external partnership opportunities. Strengthen employer retention by providing responsive service, gathering feedback, communicating impact, and identifying opportunities for deeper engagement. Develop resources and engagement pathways that make it easier for employers to understand how to connect with Seattle University. Recruiting and Career-Connected Programming - 25% Provide strategic and operational leadership for employer-facing recruiting programs and career-connected events. Collaborate with the Career Education team to ensure that employer programming complements students’ career readiness preparation and learning needs. Partner with the Albers Career Center and other university offices to coordinate employer outreach, reduce duplication, and create a consistent experience for employer partners. Recruit employers and industry professionals to participate in mock interviews, résumé reviews, mentorship programs, classroom presentations, career education workshops, and other student-learning activities. Support the development of micro-internships, project-based experiences, job-shadowing, and other lower-barrier opportunities for students who are still developing professional experience. Ensure that employer programs and recruiting activities comply with university policies, applicable employment regulations, ethical recruiting standards, and National Association of Colleges and Employers guidance. Institutional Collaboration and Experiential Learning - 10% Serve as an employer-facing resource for university initiatives related to internships, experiential learning, mentorship, workforce development, AI career readiness, and professional formation. Help identify gaps in experiential opportunity by academic program, student population, career interest, and industry. Support the development of scalable practices for connecting academic learning with employer-sponsored projects, internships, research, service, and other applied experiences. Participate in cross-campus committees and working groups related to employer engagement, experiential learning, student outcomes, and workforce readiness. Ensure employer engagement efforts complement specialized career services within the Albers School of Business and avoid unnecessary duplication. Data, Systems, Assessment, and Reporting - 10% Establish measurable goals for employer outreach, partnership development, opportunity creation, employer retention, and student engagement. Maintain accurate employer relationship, recruiting, and opportunity data within Handshake and other approved university systems. Develop dashboards, reports, and summaries that communicate employer engagement activity and impact to Career Engagement leadership and university stakeholders. Assess employer satisfaction and use feedback to continuously improve services, programs, and partnership practices. Monitor internships, jobs, recruiting activity, employer participation, and other indicators of partnership effectiveness. Collaborate with Career Engagement colleagues to connect employer engagement data with student participation and post-graduation outcomes. Perform other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in business, higher education, human resources, communications, marketing, public relations, nonprofit leadership, or a related field, or equivalent combination of education and relevant professional experience. At least five years of progressively responsible experience in employer relations, university recruiting, talent acquisition, business development, workforce development, corporate relations, alumni relations, career services, or a related field. Experience translating relationships into programs, opportunities, partnerships, or measurable outcomes. Strong written, verbal, interpersonal, presentation, and relationship-management skills. Demonstrated ability to manage multiple partnerships, projects, events, deadlines, and priorities. Ability to work collaboratively across organizational boundaries and with stakeholders who have varied goals and responsibilities. Demonstrated commitment to equity, inclusion, social justice, cultural humility, and serving diverse student populations. Ability to use data and technology to manage relationships, assess performance, and inform decisions. Availability for occasional evening or weekend programs and local travel to employer and community sites. Preferred Qualifications Experience in a college or university career center, employer-relations operation, talent acquisition function, workforce-development organization, or corporate partnership role. Knowledge of university recruiting, internship development, experiential learning, mentorship, early-career hiring, and employer engagement practices. Familiarity with customer relationship management systems, career-services technology, or employer relationship-management platforms. Experience using labor-market information and employer data to guide strategy. Familiarity with National Association of Colleges and Employers standards, career-readiness competencies, and ethical recruiting principles. Understanding of the Seattle and Puget Sound employer, nonprofit, government, and workforce-development landscape. Experience supporting fundraising, sponsorship, corporate engagement, or partnership models that include financial and nonfinancial investment. Seattle University Mission Alignment The Associate Director advances Seattle University’s mission of educating the whole person, promoting professional formation, and empowering leaders for a just and humane world. By connecting students with meaningful work, professional relationships, experiential learning, and organizations committed to positive social and economic impact, the position helps students translate their education into lives of purpose, leadership, and service. The Associate Director will ensure that employer engagement reflects Seattle University’s Jesuit values and expands access to social capital, professional networks, and career opportunities for all students. Compensation at a Glance $67,000 - $74,700 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate’s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3–6 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university’s discretion."
    },
    "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Director--Orientation-Programs_JR100372": {
      "title": "Director, Orientation Programs",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary The Director of Orientation Programs serves as the institutional leader for visioning, strategic direction, and delivery of new student orientation and transition support initiatives for all incoming undergraduate first year and transfer students across the First Hill and South Lake Union campuses. This includes attention to support of traditionally underrepresented student populations and an expansive understanding of the unique onboarding needs of each school or college’s programs at both campus locations. The Director leads the university’s efforts to continuously strengthen and align the path from deposit to matriculation. Position Description: The Director of Orientation Programs serves as the institutional leader for visioning, strategic direction, and delivery of new student orientation and transition support initiatives for all incoming undergraduate first year and transfer students across the First Hill and South Lake Union campuses. This includes attention to support of traditionally underrepresented student populations and an expansive understanding of the unique onboarding needs of each school or college’s programs at both campus locations.  The Director leads the university’s efforts to continuously s trengthen and align the path from deposit to matriculation. The Director is expected to function in a collaborative manner with a high degree of visibility and contact with students, families, staff, faculty, and administration. This role requires innovation and knowledge of leading-edge practices to enhance the experience of current student populations.  In alignment with the university’s commitment to student success, retention, and persistence, the director will use a framework of social justice and Jesuit education to deliver a comprehensive entry-year experience and celebration of key milestones. Essential Functions: Strategic Leadership and Vision: 40% Lead university-wide strategy for new student onboarding in alignment with Seattle University’s mission and institutional priorities. Assume responsibility for incoming students post-deposit up to and through the start of their first term. Design, implement, and assess in-person and online new student transition curriculum. Analyze and prepare data and metrics reports and presentations for campus partners and senior leadership in regular cadence leading up to start of each term. Strategize and deliver proactive and reactive responses to student progress through New Student Checklist tasks, with emphasis on high melt risk students. Craft and implement individualized support for unique populations, including but not limited to: Adult learners, BIPOC students, Commuter students, Exchange students, First Generation students, Honors students, International students, LGBTQ+ students, Scholar cohorts, Student Athletes, Transfer students, and Veteran and Military-Connected students. Maintain knowledge of unique onboarding needs for Cornish College of the Arts student experience. Implement behavior-driven anti-melt student and family communications plan to drive completion of essential onboarding tasks on New Student Checklist. Develop strategic plans, goals, and measurable objectives for Orientation Programs. Lead regular new student cohort check-in meetings and deliverables in collaboration with Admissions, Housing and Residence Life, and University Advising. Lead strategic collaborations with 35+ university departments to collectively deliver a seamless orientation experience Regularly conduct benchmarking with peer institutions and review research and leading-edge work in the field to evaluate for utility at Seattle University. Program and Event Coordination: 35% Manage 20+ university-wide in-person orientation events annually including one-day, multi-day, overnight, and weeklong experiences Oversee daily program and event operations including schedule, content, space reservation/setup, catering, parking/transportation, equipment rentals, campus access, contracts, staffing, technology, and more. Coordinate parent, family, and supporter onboarding, including virtual/in-person events and communications in collaboration with Parent & Family Engagement Manage design, operation, and enhancements of Orientation Portal in Slate CRM, centralizing New Student Checklist, Advising & Registration, event registration, and communications Serve as lead event coordinator for New Student Convocation in collaboration with Office of the Provost. Coordinate Early Bird Immersion early arrival programs in collaboration with relevant campus partners, supporting event operations, registrations, and scholarships Co-lead Move-In Experience Committee with staff in Housing & Residence Life Serve as university administrator for ALEKS Math Placement Assessment Design new student resource guides and online learning modules to support incoming student and family learning and preparedness. Co-coordinate advising and registration processes for incoming students in collaboration with University Advising. Collaborate on Common Text program with University Core and First-Year Academic Engagement. Partner with Marketing & Communication on digital and print design assets in alignment with university branding Establish and continuously enhance strategic integration of faculty and academic affairs across orientation programming and services. Departmental Leadership: 25% Recruit, hire, train, supervise, and evaluate staff, including one full-time administrative coordinator, two part-time graduate assistants, and 45+ undergraduate orientation leaders. Conduct assessment, evaluation, and data reporting of departmental programs. Develop multi-year, sustainable financial plans and manage departmental budget to align. Maintain breadth and depth of knowledge of operational processes across First Hill and South Lake Union campus to support event management and new student onboarding. Serve as primary speaker for all orientation events. Benchmark, calculate, and manage revenue related to orientation fees, guest fees, and other related program fees. Serve as a member of Student Success Leadership Committee. Oversee departmental web presence (website, social media) and Orientation (Guidebook) app. Promote a campus culture of shared responsibility for new student onboarding. Educate campus partners on research and best practices and collaboratively implement in a way fitting to Seattle University. Establish and sustain sponsor partnerships with community businesses. Support campus partner events through staffing presence and co-hosting programs and services. Marginal Job Functions Participate in divisional and campus committees. Represent the division and the university to external constituents, including at professional associations. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Master’s degree in higher education and five years of progressive experience in student affairs with skills in administering and educational program or a related field required OR an equivalent combination of education and experience sufficient to perform the essential functions of the job as listed above. A demonstrated commitment to inclusion and social justice in alignment with the University's mission, vision and values Effective skills in building and sustaining campus relationships and working collaboratively. Demonstrated supervisory and/or mentorship skills with students. Effective communication, interpersonal and team-building skills. Ability to work effectively one-on-one with students, staff, and faculty. An understanding of and appreciation for student development and services in a Jesuit institution of higher education. A willingness to work flexible hours including evenings and weekends. This position requires a criminal history background check. Preferred Qualifications Doctoral degree in higher education or related field and seven years working with orientation and/or transition programs. Demonstrated ability to successfully navigate the diverse interests of multiple campus stakeholders. Demonstrated leadership and staff supervisory experience. Knowledge and experience of specialized student populations listed above. Excellent verbal, written and organizational skills; capability to multi-task; demonstrated ability to manage budgets, develop and implement assessment; and strategic planning methods and reporting functions. Skilled use of technology, social media, and/or managing communication campaigns. Prior experience and training using CRM platforms. Compensation at a Glance $88,000 - $92,000 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate’s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3–6 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university’s discretion."
    },
    "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Admissions-Evaluator---International-Credential-Evaluations_JR100271": {
      "title": "Admissions Evaluator - International Credential Evaluations",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary The Admissions Evaluator – International Credential Evaluations carefully evaluates international transcripts for GPA calculations, transferable credit and major prerequisites. The Evaluator serves as a Designated School Official (DSO) and issues I-20's for international students to be able to apply for visas. As the subject matter expert in international evaluations, the Evaluator also plays a key role in functions related to admissions operations for all undergraduate applicants as part of a team of Admissions Evaluators, which includes evaluating transcripts and calculating GPA’s for domestic students, and maintaining data and applicant/record cleanliness. Position Description: International (60%) Serves as the subject matter expert for evaluating secondary and post-secondary international credentials for incoming students. This includes calculating GPAs according to grading scales from other countries, determining course equivalencies from post-secondary institutions outside the United States, and applying applicants’ previous coursework towards Seattle University’s degree requirements. Regularly reviews other components of international applications, including auditing for fulfillment of the university’s English Proficiency requirement and confirming citizenship information. Serves as a Designated School Official (DSO), which involves issuing initial I-20's for incoming international students. Works in close partnership with the International Student and Scholar Center and participates in regular DSO trainings. Communicates with students on missing materials needed for I-20 issuance. Serves as a member of the undergraduate international admissions group which includes the Sr. Assistant Director for International Recruitment and the International Transfer Admissions Counselor. Participates in regular meetings with the group and supports international functions in close collaboration with the Sr. Assistant Director for International Recruitment. Supports with monitoring emails in the general international inbox and responding to student questions as needed. General Admissions Operations (35%) Determines course equivalencies and applies applicants’ previous coursework towards Seattle University’s degree requirements and posts appropriate equivalencies of transferred credit hours awarded and current class standing in the University’s ERP and CRM systems, specifically Colleague, Workday, and Slate. Serves as an academic policy resource for incoming freshmen and transfer students regarding University Core and major transfer requirements for all undergraduate degrees and is a key contact for Transfer Admissions Counselors and designated academic liaisons throughout the University on academic policy matters related to the onboarding of undergraduate students with transferrable credit. Adds course equivalencies to the online TES database system and assists in the development of effective tools/resources for best practices in communicating transfer course equivalencies to prospective transfers. Assists in maintaining data and applicant/record cleanliness in various ways working closely with other individuals within the Admissions Operations team and Research and Analytics. These tasks may consist of editing small parts of multiple records, general audits, the scanning and assigning of documents, exporting of data for mass corrections, etc. Other (5%) Participates in office-wide committees, projects, and other duties as assigned in Undergraduate Admissions. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree . Demonstrated commitment university's mission, vision and values. One or more years of experience in detailed data entry. Ability to effectively use and quickly learn new technological systems. Strong critical thinking skills and the ability to exercise professional judgment to make informed decisions based on experience, reason and policy while considering issues of equity, fairness and historical precedent Adept at managing and prioritizing multiple tasks Ability to work both independently and collaboratively in a team environment. The ideal candidate will be a resilient and innovative problem solver, have excellent organizational abilities with acute attention to detail, and strong interpersonal skills that enable him/her to develop positive relationships with key campus partners and provide personalized attention to prospective students throughout the admissions process Preferred Prior experience in an educational environment in the areas of credential evaluation, academic advising, and/or transfer evaluations. Familiarity with international education and exposure to schooling systems in other countries. Compensation at a Glance $47,000 - $49,000 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate’s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3–6 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university’s discretion."
    },
    "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Executive-Coordinator_JR100368": {
      "title": "Executive Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary The Executive Coordinator provides high-level administrative and operational support to the Dean of the College of Science and Engineering, contributing to the effective leadership and advancement of the College. This position supports the Dean’s efforts to promote and represent the College within the University and with external constituents, including donors, alumni, corporate partners, prospective students, and other key stakeholders. The Executive Coordinator manages administrative functions within the Dean’s Office and supports special projects and initiatives as directed by the Dean. The position also serves as a key resource to faculty and staff, providing guidance and information related to University and College policies, procedures, rank and tenure processes, and business operations. The Executive Coordinator reports directly to the Dean of the College of Science and Engineering and plays an important role in fostering effective communication, coordination, and collaboration across the College. Position Description: This position supports the efforts of the dean to lead and promote the college both within the university and externally to donors, alumni, corporate partners, and prospective students, etc. The executive coordinator is responsible for administrative support duties in the Dean's Office and supports various projects as the dean requires. This position serves as a resource for faculty and staff regarding policies, procedures, rank and tenure, and the business functions of the college. This position reports to the Dean of the College of Science and Engineering.   Essential Job Functions Executive Assistant (45%)   Schedule the dean's meetings with faculty, staff, other members of the university community, donors, and visitors.   Take minutes and transcribe same for Executive Committee meetings as well as college-wide meetings, as needed.   Make travel arrangements for the dean. Communicate with the appropriate offices on campus to fulfill the dean's requests for information.    Collaborate with college’s development officer to set up donor meetings for the dean, special visits, and events.   Serve as contact for requests for college calendar postings. Oversee permissions for college listservs.   Provide administrative support for the Executive Committee, Student Advisory Council, faculty searches , rank & tenure process, annual performance reviews, sabbaticals, and other activities designated by the dean.     Event Planner (20%)   Arrange faculty/staff meetings, events and all college-wide receptions and gatherings.   Create agendas, schedules, orders of events and briefs for events as needed.   Collaborate with other college and university principals as needed for event planning.   Create and maintain invitations, name tags and RSVP lists.   Coordinate with CES, Catering and ITCS as needed.    Clerical (15%)   Keep track of forms requiring dean's signature and route them accordingly.   Maintain/update personnel files of faculty members and college roster updates.   Coordinate rank-and-tenure dossier submission and support rank -and-tenure process within the college.   Produce grade distribution reports for faculty members seeking promotion and/or tenure.   Handle paperwork for annual performance reviews, sabbatical requests, and junior faculty development leaves .    Reporting (10%)   Using university systems, collect data and produce various reports, including, but not limited to: enrollment trends, student credit hours generated, and other measures of the productivity of individual departments and the entire college.   Prepare presentations for dean.    Office Reception (10%)   Receive and direct incoming phone calls, greet visitors, and provide visitors and callers with general information.   Route mail and packages. Compensation at a Glance $52,000 - $62,000 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate’s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3–6 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university’s discretion."
    },
    "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Part-time-Jewish-Chaplain_JR100369": {
      "title": "Part-time Jewish Chaplain",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: Responsible for providing pastoral care, leading weekly student programs, stewarding program budgets, cultivating strategic partnerships with local organizations, and coordinating interfaith initiatives to strengthen Jewish life and presence on campus. This is a part-time position scheduled for 10 hours per week, 11 months per year, with July off. Essential Job Functions Accompaniment and spiritual support of Jewish students primarily, with some overlap with supporting staff and faculty (40%) Work with Jewish Student Union and other Jewish student leaders to develop religious services and gatherings on campus (20%) Oversee and develop inventory and administration of current Jewish prayer spaces (10%) Participate, collaborate and engage in inter-religious programing in partnership with University Ministry, Theology and Religious Studies, Mission Integration and the Center for Ecumenical and Interreligious Engagement (10%) Provide spiritual leadership and accompaniment of all students with a spirit of spiritual and religious inclusivity  and educate the community about Judaism (10%) Attend team and division meetings where appropriate and engage in necessary administrative tasks such as room scheduling, maintaining budget for programs and working with marketing to share and communicate offerings. (10%) Qualifications Designation as Rabbi by a Jewish religious authority An MDiv master’s degree in Jewish studies or related At least 2-3 years of experience in a university setting Familiarity and passion around Jesuit educational values Compensation at a Glance Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. $35 per hour Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate’s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3–6 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university’s discretion."
