    "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Senior-Administrative-Assistant_JR100352": {
      "title": "Senior Administrative Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: Seattle University is seeking an experienced Administrative Assistant to join the Office of the Global Engagement. The Office of Global Engagement’s mission is to foster international partnerships, support engagement through global grants, and move forward with the Global Jesuit Initiatives in Latin America, Asia Pacific and South Asia.   As Sr. Administrative Assistant, you will support the work of the Associate Vice Provost for Global Engagement and the Associate Vice Provost for Global Academic Programs by scheduling and staffing meetings, coordinating projects, arranging travel for international visitors, preparing reports and correspondence, and managing files. The ideal candidate is a dynamic professional who can handle confidential matters with professionalism, work with a positive and solutions-oriented mindset, and demonstrate strong judgment, tact, and diplomacy. Support for the Associate Vice Provosts (50%) Manage calendars and incoming external calls for the Associate Vice Provosts, ensuring sufficient work time and time for meetings and events. Coordinate travel arrangements and itineraries. Assist with emails as directed. Communicate with internal and external constituents, drafting responses to correspondence, where appropriate. Submit, track, and reconcile expenditures. Process invoices, requisitions, reimbursements, and payments for the office. Work in partnership with the Provost, Senior Vice Provosts, Vice Provosts, and Deans, and their office staff on all business needs. Support for the Office of Global Engagement (30%) Manage meeting and event logistics, including distribution of materials, room reservations, A/V, technical support, and catering. Prepare meeting minutes.  Coordinate travel arrangements and itineraries for visiting international scholars and guests. Maintain email distribution lists and partnership databases.  Prepare correspondence, newsletters, the annual report, and other documents. Manage the Office of Global Engagement files and SharePoint website.  Support the Global Jesuit Initiatives as directed by the Special Assistant to the Provost for Strategic Initiatives. Support for the Center for Language and International Communication (CLIC) (20%) Submit, track, and reconcile expenditures. Process invoices, requisitions, reimbursements, and payments for the office. Maintain CLIC alumni database and photo archives. Coordinate logistics for the CLIC Language Partner Program. Assist with Global Engagement grant-funded activities (e.g., career workshops) in partnership with campus offices. Critical Success Factors Confidentiality, diplomacy, tact, discretion, and collaboration are required. Courteous and efficient handling of all contacts. Diplomacy in responding to domestic and international visitors, phone calls, and customer service issues.  Willingness to receive work from executive staff and senior level administrators, often with little or no instructions or established practices or procedures.  Comfort working independently and making judicious decisions based on thorough knowledge of functional area policies and procedures.  Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree or an equivalent combination of education and experience sufficient to successfully perform the essential duties of the job. 2-3 years of experience providing successful administrative support. Demonstrated ability to multi-task and prioritize projects. Strong organizational and planning skills. Excellent verbal and written communication skills required including experience preparing, reviewing, and editing correspondence and documents. Advanced knowledge of all Microsoft Office products.  Quickly learn new systems and adapt to existing practices, while also looking for efficiencies in existing processes. Reliability. Attention to detail. Positive attitude and willingness to pitch in as needed. Preferred Qualifications Master's degree.  Experience supporting a senior executive. Knowledge and experience in higher education. Knowledge and experience in international relations.  Thorough knowledge of a university’s organization and operation. Compensation at a Glance Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. $42,900 - $52,000 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate’s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3–6 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university’s discretion."
    },
    "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Director-Web-Strategy-and-Development_JR100360": {
      "title": "Director Web Strategy and Development",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: Primary Responsibilities Digital Strategy & Product Leadership Develop and execute a comprehensive, multi-year web and digital experience strategy aligned with Seattle University's strategic and marketing priorities. Lead major website redesigns, platform migrations, integrations, and digital transformation initiatives. Partner with university leadership and stakeholders to identify opportunities for digital experiences to improve recruitment, enrollment, engagement, fundraising, and institutional reputation. Establish scalable approaches for integrating newly acquired, affiliated, or evolving institutional web properties into the broader Seattle University digital ecosystem. Web Platforms & Technical Strategy Provide strategic and technical leadership for the University's web technology ecosystem, including Terminalfour, WordPress, Drupal, SharePoint. Establish platform architecture, configuration, development standards, release processes, integrations, APIs, and technical governance. Evaluate the long-term role of existing platforms and recommend consolidation, migration, modernization, or replacement when appropriate. Partner with ITS on infrastructure, authentication, integrations, security, cloud services, data architecture, and enterprise technology standards. Oversee development environments, source control, Git workflows, deployment processes, CDNs, performance optimization, monitoring, and quality assurance. Ensure web platforms remain scalable, maintainable, secure, performant, and resilient. User Experience, Content Strategy & Search Engine Optimization Lead user experience, information architecture, content strategy, navigation, search, SEO, and digital discovery. Improve audience journeys, conversion, engagement, findability, and task completion through research and continuous optimization. Establish scalable content models, structured data, taxonomy, and SEO practices supporting both traditional and AI-powered discovery. Establish technical and content SEO standards across institutional websites. Optimize University content for traditional search engines as well as evolving AI-powered search, answer engines, and generative discovery experiences. Oversee structured data, metadata, taxonomy, schema, content relationships, and other technologies that improve discoverability. Analytics, Measurement & Optimization Establish a comprehensive digital measurement strategy for university web properties. Oversee implementation and governance of web analytics, tag management, dashboards, conversion tracking, and related measurement technologies. Develop reporting systems that connect website activity to institutional outcomes including recruitment, enrollment, engagement, and conversion. Use quantitative and qualitative data to prioritize development and content improvements. Establish key performance indicators for digital platforms and communicate performance, opportunities, and risks to university leadership. Accessibility, Privacy, Security & Governance Ensure University digital properties meet applicable WCAG, ADA, privacy, security, and regulatory requirements. Establish University-wide web governance, publishing, accessibility, quality assurance, and development standards. Maintain processes supporting emergency communications, monitoring, incident response, and business continuity. Provide training, documentation, and support for the University's community of web editors. University Partnerships & Vendor Management Serve as a strategic advisor to university leadership, schools, colleges, departments, and administrative units on web strategy, technology, digital experience, and emerging technologies. Lead relationships with external digital agencies, development partners, consultants, hosting providers, SaaS vendors, and technology partners. Manage contracts, scopes of work, budgets, renewals, vendor performance, and technology investments. Coordinate multiple internal and external teams working across the University's digital ecosystem. Partner closely with ITS, Enrollment Management, Graduate Studies, Advancement, Academic Affairs, and other university stakeholders. Leadership & Partnership Lead a talented multidisciplinary team spanning web development, content strategy, web support, UX/UI, visual design, and student employees. Manage external agencies, consultants, technology vendors, contracts, budgets, and strategic partnerships. Collaborate across Marketing, ITS, Enrollment Management, Graduate Studies, Advancement, Academic Affairs, schools, and colleges. Advocate appropriate staffing, technology, budget, and resources necessary to support the University's evolving digital ecosystem. Emerging Technology Develop and lead a responsible strategy for incorporating artificial intelligence and agentic technologies into the University's web and digital operations. Identify opportunities for AI-powered search, conversational interfaces, intelligent content discovery, personalization, recommendation systems, and audience experiences across university websites. Partner with ITS and university leadership to ensure emerging technologies are adopted responsibly and in accordance with institutional policies and risk-management standards. Minimum Qualifications Master's degree in an applicable field of study or equivalent combination of education and significant professional experience. 7+ years of progressive experience in web, digital technology, digital experience, or related fields, including significant leadership responsibility. Demonstrated experience developing and executing enterprise web and digital strategies. Experience managing complex, multi-site and multi-platform web ecosystems. Experience with enterprise content management and digital experience platforms such as Terminalfour, Hannon Hill’s Cascade Server, WordPress, Drupal, and SharePoint. Experience leading large-scale website redesigns, migrations, platform implementations, and digital transformation initiatives. Strong understanding of modern web architecture, APIs, integrations, cloud platforms, CDNs, Git-based development, CI/CD, and deployment workflows. Strong knowledge of front-end web technologies including HTML, CSS, and JavaScript and familiarity with modern development frameworks and practices. Experience with web analytics, tag management, SEO, structured data, conversion measurement, and digital performance reporting. Strong understanding of WCAG accessibility requirements, privacy, security, responsive design, UX/UI, and web governance. Demonstrated ability to manage vendors, agencies, contracts, budgets, and complex cross-functional initiatives. Demonstrated experience directly managing and developing a multidisciplinary digital team (for example, web development, content strategy, and UX/design), including budget and vendor oversight. Excellent project and product management skills with the ability to manage multiple strategic priorities and cross-functional initiatives simultaneously. Excellent interpersonal, communication, and relationship-building skills, with demonstrated ability to influence and collaborate across technical teams, marketers, content creators, senior leadership, and distributed stakeholders. Demonstrated commitment to Seattle University's mission, vision, values, and commitment to inclusive excellence. Preferred Qualifications Master's degree in web technology, digital strategy, computer science, information systems, communications, marketing technology, or a related discipline. More than 10 years of experience leading web and digital technology programs. Experience leading web and digital technology programs at a large, decentralized, multi-brand, or multi-entity institution or organization. Higher education digital strategy or technology leadership experience. Experience evaluating, consolidating, or migrating enterprise CMS/DXP platforms across a large, multi-site ecosystem. Experience with enterprise search platforms, structured content, personalization, marketing technology, and CRM integrations. Experience developing governance frameworks for artificial intelligence and other emerging digital technologies, and translating them into practical, scalable programs that advance institutional goals. Experience leading larger, more complex multidisciplinary digital teams spanning development, content strategy, UX, analytics, and digital operations, including external partners and agencies.   Compensation at a Glance $120,000 - $130,000 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate’s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3–6 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university’s discretion."
    },
    "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Budget-Manager--CCA_JR100415-1": {
      "title": "Budget and Operations Manager, CCA",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary Reporting to the Dean of Cornish College of the Arts, the Budget and Operations Manager will oversee all budget analysis, planning and reporting, as well as grant budgeting in the College. This position will also serve as a facilities liaison for the College and work with Facilities regarding maintenance and contracts for South Lake Union campus buildings. Position Description: Essential Job Functions Budgeting (45%) Work with the College and University leadership to develop and submit an annual operating budget and make strategic budgetary projections and recommendations. Develop annual budget based on strategic plan. Review salaries, benefit rates and non-salary budgets. Project faculty FTE based on curriculum offerings, coordinate course assignments and budgets with shared faculty in other colleges, and update budget projections based on enrollment changes.    Develop budgets for new programs and grant proposals and prioritize list of funding requests. Develop new funding requests for current programs, re-allocate budgets among departments as necessary. Review and approve expenditures, travel reimbursements and purchase requisitions for all school budgets, including salary and operating budgets, institutional, endowed funds and gift accounts. Oversee purchasing for departmental annual capital equipment allocations, including developing request forms and assisting with purchases. Manage financial aspects of job postings and position descriptions for school staff, temporary employee hiring and supplemental pay postings.   Reporting and Reconciliations (25%) Reconcile monthly faculty and staff payroll report for accuracy. Analysis of faculty salary encumbrance reports including: FTE comparison with prior years, reconcile adjunct salary pool with actual adjunct contracts, and monitor labor charges to sabbatical, medical and release budgets. Update and reconcile commitments from gift accounts. Reconcile quarterly FRX grant reports. Reconcile graduate tuition splits with outside partner 501 (c) (3) organizations. Monitor charges to all budgets. Faculty Contracting (15%) Calculate across-the-board faculty salary increases. Convert faculty merit points to salary merit increases. Review faculty salary percentages based on FTE, phased retirements and sabbaticals. Update master worksheet for all full-year contracted faculty including budget labor charges. Submit adjunct faculty contract information to Faculty Services each quarter. Ensure all adjunct contracts are received by contract deadline. Administrative Operations (15%) Meet with facilities regarding SLU building safety and maintenance. Review contracts for compliance with budget. Review active grant proposals for budget compliance and potential cost sharing. Represent College on select university committees such as directed. Requirements Bachelor's degree in business or related field plus at least 3 years’ experience in a relevant role, OR an equivalent combination of education and experience to successfully perform the essential duties of the job (MBA preferred) Must have the capability to oversee multiple budgets, generate spreadsheets, records and reports preparation, and word-processing experience. Must have excellent interpersonal skills and the ability to interact with diverse individuals and groups effectively. Need strong oral presentation skills and ability to produce complex reports with clarity for diverse audiences. Ability to maintain strict confidentiality. Must have excellent time management and organizational skills to complete multiple tasks concurrently and meet deadlines. All candidates must show a demonstrated commitment to inclusion and the university's mission, vision, and values. Compensation at a Glance $80,000 - $89,800 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate’s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3–6 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university’s discretion."
    },
    "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Admissions-Counselor_JR100481": {
      "title": "Admissions Counselor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary The Admissions Counselor will join a dynamic undergraduate admissions team that represents Seattle University's Jesuit academic and co-curricular opportunities to prospective students, their families and supporters, and the high school college counselor community. This position works primarily with first-time-in-college (FTIC) students applying directly from high school. However, the counseling team supports all prospective students, including First-Year, Transfer, Arts, Pre-College, and Post-Baccalaureate, by providing advising and support through email and phone communication, appointments, events on and off campus, and other outreach programs as needed. Position Description: Recruitment Management - 45% Provide information about the University's academic opportunities to prospective students, families, counselors, advisors, and other stakeholders through presentations, emails, calls, and one-on-one conversation. Plan, develop, and implement on and off campus recruitment travel and activities primarily in assigned geographic regions. Meet with prospective students through various channels, including high school visits, campus interviews, information sessions, and workshops. Act as a connection point between students and other campus stakeholders (i.e. faculty, financial aid, registrar, etc.) to meet undergraduate enrollment goals. Engage in efforts to make higher education more accessible, equitable, and inclusive; participate in working groups to embed these efforts in job responsibilities. Approximately 10 to 12 weeks of in state and out of state travel. Application Review and Recommendations - 25% Review, evaluate, and provide initial undergraduate admission recommendations based on university criteria and artistic standards (where necessary) Assist students in submitting materials to complete their applications and advise through questions about best-fit majors, changing majors, and updating start terms. Event Attendance - 15% Support and actively participate in programs and events engaging prospective First Year, Transfer, Post Baccalaureate, and Pre College students, their families, community organizations, college counselors, and advisors. Attend major admission events such as preview days and admitted student days. Evening and weekend work required, especially in fall and spring. Other Duties - 15% Manage and work on assigned project areas throughout the year. Other duties as assigned. Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Education Requirements Minimum: Bachelor's Degree (4-year) in a relevant field. Experience Minimum of 1 year of professional work experience, or a combination of education and experience sufficient to successfully perform the duties and responsibilities of the job. Minimum Qualifications Must possess well-developed organizational skills. Confidence in public speaking to both large and small groups. Strong oral and written communication skills. Ability to establish and maintain cooperative and effective working relationships with others. A commitment to diversity, equity and inclusion, and supporting diverse student populations. Ability and willingness to travel and work evenings and weekends as necessary. Willingness to learn quickly, ask questions, and accept constructive feedback. Valid driver's license required. Preferred Qualifications Speaks, writes, and reads one of the following languages: Spanish, Vietnamese, Somali, Mandarin, Tagalog, Amharic, or Tigrinya, and is interested in utilizing language skills to serve prospective students and families in the college search process. Knowledge of Microsoft Office products and federal and state privacy laws. Experience using an ERP and CRM, specifically Workday and Technolutions Slate CRM. Knowledge of financial aid trends and issues. Experience working with diverse young adult and youth populations. Physical Demands This position performs work in an office environment. Frequency values: Never; Seldom (1–10%, 0–1 hour); Occasional (11–33%, 1–3 hours); Frequent (34–66%, 3–6 hours); Constant (67–100%, not restricted).   Travel - Frequent Lifting boxes or other items for travel and events - Occasional Compensation at a Glance $52,000 - $53,000 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate’s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3–6 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university’s discretion."
    },
    "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Senior-Administrative-Assistant_JR100376": {
      "title": "Senior Administrative Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-22\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary To provide administrative office support services to the chair, faculty and students of the Department of Modern Languages and Cultures. This position is full time September through June and part time (20 hours per week) during July and August. Position Description: Modern Languages and Cultures Department Support(90%) Serve as front-line support and knowledgeable resource for all students, faculty, and guests of the Modern Languages and Cultures Department, maintaining an open-door policy and welcoming environment. Answer incoming calls, e-mail messages and questions from students and general public. Provide multi-faceted programmatic and operational support, including classroom assignments, office assignments, key requisitions and making copies of essential department projects as requested. Support departmental meetings, send reminders, and record, write, and distribute minutes. Track that tasks assigned during meetings are accomplished. Developing and/or updating department promotional materials and keeping copies available at all times. Troubleshooting faculty and student requests. Maintain confidential student records, process student Registrar forms. Maintain department statistical data including annual lists of active minors and majors and keep current student email lists and academic advising handouts. Use student and learning management systems and internal data delivery applications needed for registering students, auditing degree requirements, and generating admissions and enrollment reports. Manage Challenge Exams schedules and results during the four quarters of the year, to be sent to the Office of the Registrar as well as communicated to students. Maintain accuracy and relevance of department website. Update regularly with student’s spotlight section, changes in any language and events. Coordinate logistics for department events including creating flyers, securing room reservations, catering orders, and guest parking plus assisting with advertising and overseeing day-of-event activities. Hire, train, and supervise a part-time student office assistant. Assist with the hiring and interviews of new faculty. Manage on-boarding of part-time faculty including requesting and tracking Letters of Appointment, and updating schedules with the names of new instructors. Assist faculty with departmental computer refresh needs. Prepare Modern Languages and Cultures Department Program Review documents and assist in any tasks related to completing the process. Assist with annual academic catalog updates and support ongoing program assessment. Scheduling Duties   Coordinate with department chair to assemble and maintain quarterly schedule of classes. Enter and track quarterly course schedule, make periodic reviews and changes as needed, and monitor the schedules to make sure that the changes are reflected on the system prior to the opening registration date. Prepare and submit curriculum forms. Update faculty teaching assignments. Work to resolve scheduling conflicts and room requests. Monitor wait lists and manually register students when needed. Track enrollment caps during student registration periods. Maintain booking calendar in Outlook for shared department conference room. Financial Activity Management Prepare requests for all department and faculty led study abroad programs expenditures and reimbursement requests including assigning correct activity and accounting codes and monitoring purchases to assure they are within budget limitations and in accordance with University polices. Assist with faculty reimbursements through ProcureSU. Manage Procard account for department. Process honorarium requests. Manage and purchase office supplies. Other duties as assigned. College Committee Support or Other Approved Service (10%) Provide administrative and logistical support for College committee activities, including: Support for Faculty-Staff Senate (or similar) Organize and attend virtual meetings (approximately 9 standing meetings). Prepare and post meeting minutes. Provide logistical and event support including catering coordination (approximately 4 events annually). College Committee Elections Coordinate the annual College committee election process in collaboration with FSS senators and the Dean’s Office. Identify vacant committee positions; prepare election communications; solicit and track nominations; develop and administer Qualtrics ballots; and communicate election results. Qualifications High school diploma and three or more years of previous administrative support experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience sufficient to successfully perform the essential functions of the job. Demonstrated ability to form and maintain productive working relationships with faculty, staff, and students from diverse backgrounds. Customer service driven with self-directed problem-solving skills. Strong and professional written and oral communication skills to be able to proof and correct spelling, grammar, and punctuation in any department promotional materials or communications. Proven ability to work in a busy office environment, with frequent interruptions and competing deadlines, while maintaining a high degree of accuracy, attention to detail, and a warm and welcoming environment. Proven ability to handle confidential information with a high degree of tact and sound judgment. Experience handling the logistics of setting up meetings and other events, which may include guest parking, advertising, and catering plus overseeing day-of-event activities. Intermediate level PC-based computer skills, with strong proficiency using Microsoft Windows and Office software including Excel, Outlook, PowerPoint, and Word. Preferred Qualifications Bachelor’s degree. Proficiency in another language. Experience working in a previous administrative support position in a university setting, with familiarity using student and learning management systems. Compensation at a Glance $22.80 per hour - $23.86 per hour Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate’s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3–6 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university’s discretion."
    },
    "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/SUYI-RAMP-at-WMS-Mentor_JR100237-1": {
      "title": "SUYI RAMP at WMS Mentor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-21\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nMonths Per Year: Work Model: Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary Position Description:   DEPARTMENT INFORMATION   Sundborg Center for Community Engagement, Seattle University   901 12th Ave Seattle, WA 98122   Contact: Center for Community Engagement   cce@seattleu.edu   The Sundborg Center for Community Engagement (CCE) connects campus and community to pursue a more just and humane world. The CCE is the hub for the Seattle University Youth Initiative (SUYI). The Youth Initiative is a long-term commitment by Seattle University to unite SU and the wider community to support the social emotional growth and academic growth of youth living in SUYI neighborhoods. Our vision is to significantly transform Seattle University and the SUYI neighborhood and, in so doing, become a national model of campus-community engagement.   The Sundborg Center’s 2030 strategic plan calls for turning academic thought into action to leverage Seattle University’s faculty, staff, and students to partner with our wider community to transform Seattle University’s relationship with central Seattle by expanding trusting partnerships and deepening learning to advance justice, remove institutional barriers, and educate changemakers. Through the plan the Center is pursuing a multi-dimensional strategic approach that focuses on program development, building capacity, and addressing systemic conditions. Learn more about the Sundborg Center for Community Engagement’s new 2030 Vision and Strategic Directions in our Annual Report .   Job Description – Mentor at Washington Middle School’s Redhawk Academic Mentoring Program (RAMP)   Position Summary : A Redhawk Academic Mentor provides weekly in-person one-on-one academic and social emotional mentoring with students in grades 6, 7, and 8. Mentors will engage in this work on-site at Washington Middle School (WMS). Mentors build relationships and belonging with scholars so that they feel more connected to school and engagement in their daily lives. The position also supports specific classrooms.   Responsibilities:   Effectively develop and maintain authentic relationships with scholars, keeping detailed records of each session, participating in all personal and professional development, trainings, meetings, and effectively communicating with Washington Middle School Site Manager, Graduate Assistant at WMS, and Team Leader.   Responsibilities Include:   Work with team members to ensure that all scholars receive a healthy, positive, and consistent experience in the RAMP program.   Effectively communicate with Washington Middle School Site Manager, Graduate Assistant at WMS, Team Leader, peers, scholars, and families.   Engage in working with team lead to plan weekly sessions and activities for scholars if working in after school programming.   Engage with and in personal and professional development requirements.   On time and effective submission of proper communication and documentation is required.   Commit to a full academic year.   Qualifications Required:   Able to fully participate in 1-2 mentoring sessions, a weekly small team meeting, and monthly professional development requirements.   Willingness to grow mentoring skills and practices.   A willingness to embody positive language and positive accountability practices for teammates and scholars.   Able to interact with scholars of various social and economic backgrounds in an academic setting.   Able and willing to demonstrate effective and culturally relevant mentoring practices.   Due to the relational nature of this work, an individual working in this capacity must be dependable and reliable with communication and presence on site.   Willingness to continually develop social justice and racial equity lens through informal and formal training opportunities in accordance with the CCE’s commitment to anti-racist practices.   What You Receive:   Valuable experience working with and supporting youth using healing centered approaches.   Supportive environment to try and grow new skills.   A community (team) that nurtures belonging.   Current Hiring and Training Plan:   Mentoring will be started slowly in November and officially start in January. Professional development training and on-site orientation will begin in November. Mentors are expected to maintain a similar schedule during all 3 quarters.   The Mentors at WMS will report to the Washington Middle School Site Manager at the Center for Community Engagement. Mentors will work a maximum of 10 hours a week at $21.30 an hour.   Application Instructions & Contact Information:   Please apply through Handshake by submitting your resume and cover letter. A cover letter is not required but preferred. Contact Kendall Johansen, Washington Middle School Site Manager, with questions. Kjohansen1@seattleu.edu   SU Career & Leadership Competencies: Communication Collaboration & Teamwork Critical Thinking Creativity & Innovation Self-Awareness S ocial Justice Engagement Compensation at a Glance $21.30 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate’s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3–6 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university’s discretion."
    },
    "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Liturgical-Musician_JR100422": {
      "title": "Liturgical Musician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 months per year, 10 hours per week Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary The Part-time Liturgical Musician is part of the University Ministry team and reports to the Associate Director for University Liturgy, Worship, and Formation. The Liturgical Musician is responsible for planning the music for weekly Sunday liturgies and annual University Liturgies.  Position Description: Liturgical Music Planning and Preparation (30%) Select and prepare appropriate liturgical music for Sunday Masses, Holy Days, and occasional special services (e.g., memorial Masses, Advent/Lenten services). Use  Gather Hymnal Fourth Edition (GIA)  as the primary source, with supplemental music from  Catholic Oregon Press (OCP)  or other approved resources. Collaborate with the Associate Director for Liturgy, Worship, and Faith Formation, the Program Manager for Liturgy, and community liturgical leaders to ensure selections reflect the liturgical season and context. Accompaniment (30%) Provide  piano accompaniment (preferred)  or guitar for Sunday liturgies and other scheduled services. Rehearse with student musicians or cantors prior to Mass as needed. Choir Leadership (30%) Coordinate and support choir participation during Mass. Lead pre-Mass warmups or brief rehearsals. Collaboration and Communication (10%) Coordinate with student musicians and cantors in preparation for liturgies. Attend brief seasonal planning meetings to align music with liturgical themes and pastoral goals. Additional Responsibilities – Support as Needed Maintain or update a  simple log of seasonal music use  to assist with long-term planning. Support music coordination for  special university liturgies  (e.g., Baccalaureate, Mass of the Holy Spirit). Assist in preparing or submitting music selections for  worship aids or hymn board. These tasks are  not core expectations  but may be requested or supported collaboratively with other university ministry staff: Maintain and organize the  physical choral library  (filing cabinet in the University Ministry Office). Help catalog  digital music resources  (PDFs, planning files) within Microsoft Teams or shared folders. Ensure  basic copyright awareness , especially when reproducing non-hymnal music sel Compensation at a Glance $35 per hour Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate’s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3–6 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university’s discretion."
